\begin{abstract}
Joint-embedding predictive architectures (JEPAs) learn representations by predicting the embedding of one part of an input from the embedding of another, without negative pairs or reconstruction.
They avoid collapse through heuristics---predictor design and scale, masking, and a stop-gradient or slowly moving target encoder---that lack a common explanation.
We analyse the training dynamics near the collapsed solution, where each direction of representation space is grown by a driving force, the predictive signal shared by context and target, and shrunk by decay, from weight decay and from the self-prediction pressure of the loss.
With weight decay the collapsed point is stable, so collapse is not an instability: at a standard initialisation, with an order-one predictor and a small encoder, a direction survives only if it grows to capture the predictor before the predictor's gain decays out of the direction's growth window.
The resulting criterion has no fitted constants; it predicts which directions survive, and hence the rank, places the collapse threshold in weight decay far below the known static one, and states, for linear JEPAs, how predictor scale, learning rate and depth, the target update and the masking ratio enter.
On \EoneBRuns{} held-out, pre-registered linear-JEPA runs, the criterion predicts the rank at convergence with mean absolute error \EoneBAlignedMae{} where its assumptions hold (static threshold: \EoneBAlignedBoundMae{}), and complete collapse at weight decays of \EoneBLamCollapseTenth--\EoneBLamCollapseOne{} against the static \EoneBStaticLam{}; it over-predicts the rank of deep predictors, which a post-hoc correction of the capture condition reduces from \EoneCMaeDepthOne{} to \EoneCMae{} on a second held-out test (identity-initialised factors), and is at most partly supported under AdamW.
On \EtwoRuns{} pre-registered masking runs, the race computed from the statistics the masks leave predicts the rank with error \EtwoCycMae{} for cyclic masks under population training (static threshold: \EtwoCycBoundMae{}; SGD: \EtwoSgdMae{}), but not for a tanh encoder under random masks.
In an I-JEPA-style ViT a pre-registered rule from the forces at initialisation orders the rank of an SGD arm (Spearman \EthreeRhoT{}, carried by its weight-decay sweep) but misplaces the weight-decay transition $\EthreeLocFactor\times$ and fails under AdamW; the predictor, EMA and masking effects do not transfer.
\end{abstract}
